ade and  then soon after that he died and that  soon after that Robin died but the book  survived still at Sony and Sony called  and said are you still interested so on  spec I wrote a script and got Joaquin on  the line to see if he'd be interested  sent in the book and we both went to  Sony with the new script and the book  and then they said no we're not  interested so but nicely they let us  take it to Amazon  there was a quirkiness and a lightness  to the music so how did you come to  think of this and what you wanted to  tell with the score well a lot of that  has to do with gus-gus doesn't like to  play things necessarily and what would  seem like the obvious way and he likes  to kind of mix things up a lot so I  already knew that from working with them  from many times that you know he may not  want to play really sentimental where it  seems like it would be and he likes to  take things from one place and try he  likes to experiment a lot which is why  it's so fun  collaborating with Gus was the book a  good guide for you did it yes did you  balance this is before and after stories  in his life  as the book tells them well no and I  think the in the book is ten chapters I  think we concentrate on only three of  those ten chapters mainly the middle  chapter about Donnie and his his working  for his own recovery the beginning we  have a little bit of his his his in the  beginning of the book he writes about  his car crash we get into the cartooning  but we don't really go into the cartoon  world which is a large part of that the  end of the book  there's this childhood we leave out so  yeah we there was it was really like  just a section of the book you two have  worked on projects together when did  this relationship click create a kind of  synergy to die for it was kind of like  one of those funny random things where  my girlfriend at the time I was 23 years  ago yeah and she knew Gus and that was  Caroline Thompson the writer and she  came over one day said you know would I  just met where did you guys where did  you guys meet I forget she came back we  had the same agent John Brown he had the  same age and said would you like like to  meet him I go yeah [ __ ] yeah you know I  was a huge fan of Drugstore Cowboy so I  say whatever he's doing I'd love to do  it and so to die for we just jumped in  and started doing it and how was it an  awkward collaboration or did you click  at once that one was funny you just  right well I mean I I thought that in my  the only time that I'd worked with the  composer up into that point like a an  official composer because Katie Lange  did even cowgirls get the blues elliot  goldenthal had done Drugstore Cowboy but  sort of from New York so from afar and  so I thought he'd just he what you do is  you just send the cut to the composer  and they just send back the the  composition the composed film and I was  basically trying to do that I was  sending you the VHS and said yeah how  does that work tell us when you're done  i meanwhile was kind of in the other  side you know at that was probably I've  done 30 or 40 films and I was used to  like the director will you know  everything has to come from them so I  just kept sending him some saying well  which you have to choose he goes well I  don't know what do you remember going  you were living in Topanga so I would go  to Topanga and listen to the choices she  was a a B C or D and then and choose C  and then you go like well you should  listen to B again  once you do this when you when you sit  down to edit and you have so many  choices before you in telling this story  what what was it you didn't want us to  know about John or at least not to  express in the film well I mean there  was lots of ways to kind of approach the  story of about of a guy who was a great  success in his cartooning and one of  them was the story of that success and  like the the step by step advances he  made in the cartooning world the  publishing world and there 